## INTRODUCTION
Water scarcity and quality degradation due to the contamination of harmful ions have become a worldwide crisis in the 21st century, and thus there is an urgent demand to remove trace contaminants of harmful ions from polluted water. A large number of water-soluble ionic contaminants inherently exist in their oxyanion forms, such as $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}{}^{2-}.$ , $\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{SeO}}_{4}^{2-}{\mathrm{;}}$ , $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-}$ , $\mathrm{PO_{4}}^{3-},$ , and ${\mathrm{AsO}}_{4}^{3-}$ .1,2 Compared to the cationic pollutants that can be easily removed by adsorption or precipitation,3 oxyanions have higher solubility and mobility and transfer easily in the environment because the surface of soil or mineral is usually negatively charged due to its low isoelectric point, which makes the oxyanions often poorly sorbed.4
Among the harmful oxyanions, $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ has been identified as one of the 17 chemicals posing the greatest threat to humans and has also been included in the group A of human carcinogens due to its carcinogenicity, mutagenicity, and mobility.5 According to the regulations of the World Health Organization (WHO), the highest level of total Cr allowed in drinking water is $0.05\mathrm{mg/L},$ which has been adopted by European Union and China.6−8 To meet such a strict criterion, methods for the efficient removal of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ from aqueous environmental systems are highly needed.
So far, several methods for removal of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ and other oxyanion pollutants from aqueous solution have been reported, including chemical precipitation,9 adsorption,10 photocatalytic reduction ,11 and membrane filtration.12 However, most of these methods have the disadvantages of high costs, complicated operating processes, or low removal efficiencies, especially at low concentrations of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ . Compared to these methods, anion exchange is considered as a cost-effective, simple, and highly efficient method for removal of oxyanion pollutants at low levels and point-of-use applications.13−15
Significant efforts have been made in the past two decades to prepare anion exchangers for removal of oxyanion pollutants.16−19 Anion exchange resins (AERs) with aminofunctional groups are the most commonly used anion exchangers and show good performance for oxyanion removal.20−22 However, they still face many challenges in practical application because of the high cost and limited thermal and chemical stability originating from their organic nature. Recently, cationic metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively investigated and outstanding results have been obtained in the fields of oxyanion pollutant removal and catalysis.23−28 Nevertheless, the commercialization of MOFs is still hampered by their high cost and limited stability in aqueous solution.
To address these shortcomings, a series of cationic inorganic frameworks exhibiting enhanced thermal and hydrolytic stability has been prepared.29,30 Notably, layered double hydroxides (LDHs) consisting of cationic brucite-type layers charge balanced by interlayer anions have received extensive attention as the next generation of inorganic anion exchangers.31,32 LDHs have shown excellent removal efficiency for a variety of harmful oxyanions including chromate,3 phosphate,34 arsenate,35 and selenate.36 However, hightemperature calcination (usually at or above $450\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\$ ) is usually required before their usage, which is a heavy energy consumption process.37 In addition, other disadvantages for LDHs include the slow adsorption kinetics and poor selectivity, especially in the presence of bicarbonate and carbonate.38
In the past decade, a series of hydroxides and fluorides of heavy p-block metals that possess 2D cationic inorganic layers has also been reported.39−42 The interlayer anions can be exchanged, thus allowing for the removal of oxyanion pollutants from a solution. Nevertheless, the heavy metal nature of such layered materials limits their practical application. Meanwhile, a series of layered rare-earth hydroxides with a 2D cationic inorganic layer has also been synthesized in which $\mathrm{Y}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{5}\mathrm{Cl}{\cdot}1.{\bar{5}}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ has been used for removing SeO42− and SeO32− from aqueous solution.43,44 However, the use of lanthanides in large doses restricts their practical use. Notre Dame thorium borate-1 (NDTB-1), the first three-dimensional (3D) inorganic cationic extended framework, could selectively remove $^{\mathsf{o o}}\mathrm{TcO_{4}}^{-}$ from radioactive waste in the presence of a large excess of competing anions.45−47 However, the radioactive nature of thorium in the cationic framework of NDTB-1 would seriously limit its further application. Thus, it is highly desirable to seek stable and cost-effective materials with pure inorganic cationic frameworks, high capacities, fast kinetics, and good selectivity toward toxic oxyanions.
Recently, we reported a new type of layered cationic aluminum oxyhydroxide, JU-111, which was hydrothermally synthesized with low-cost raw materials.48 Such a 2D material exhibits extraordinary performance for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal even in the presence of a large excess of $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ and has a broad working $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ range, which is promising for toxic metal oxyanion remediation.
In our earlier study, we synthesized aluminoborate with a pure inorganic cationic framework, namely BAC(10) $\mathrm{'}[\mathrm{H}_{0.4}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{B}\bar{\mathrm{O}}_{4.5}]\mathrm{Cl}_{0.4}\mathrm{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ .49 The cationic framework of BAC(10) is built up from triangular $\mathrm{BO}_{3},$ tetrahedral $\mathrm{BO}_{4},$ and octahedra ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ whose positive charges are balanced by $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ anions. The water adsorption of 20 wt $9\%$ BAC(10) is comparable to that of zeolites, indicating the open-framework nature of BAC(10). Thermogravimetric analysis−mass spectrometry (TGA−MS) and titration analyses confirmed the existence of freely movable $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ in the cationic framework of BAC(10), which can be exchanged by $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ . This indicates that BAC(10) is a promising candidate for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal. Unfortunately, the structure of BAC(10) is difficult to be solved due to the ultrathin sheet-like morphology of the material.
In this context, we made further investigations on the structure of BAC(10) and its capture ability for $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{WI})}}$ from aqueous solution. The structure of the $\dot{\mathrm{{Cr}}}(\mathrm{{VI}})@\mathrm{{BAC}}(1\$ 0) sample could be successfully solved by continuous rotation electron diffraction (cRED), a data collection protocol of 3D electron diffraction including crystal tracking. The adsorption kinetics, isotherm, selectivity, removal efficiency at low concentrations, and reusability of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ on BAC(10) were systematically studied using a batch method. In addition, the removal efficiency of other oxyanion pollutants such as $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{\overline{{{3-}}}}$ was also investigated. The $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ oxyanion removal ability of BAC(10) was compared with that of other types of commercial cationic sorbents such as LDHs and AER, which clearly demonstrated its advantages in terms of adsorption capacity and adsorption kinetics. To the best of our knowledge, the 2D cationic framework aluminoborate creates a new paradigm in the removal of harmful oxyanions from water, which may open up new possibilities for its application in many other aspects.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Synthesis and Characterization of BAC(10)
Highly crystalline aluminoborate BAC(10) was hydrothermally synthesized from a mixture of boric acid, anhydrous aluminum trichloride, and ammonium hydroxide at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The detailed information on synthesis is provided in the Supporting Information.
Figure 1 shows the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of as-synthesized and calcined BAC(10) at 150, 200,
250, and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . As can be seen, the intensity of the characteristic diffraction peaks of BAC(10) decreases with the increase of heating temperature. When the heating temperature reaches $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the strongest diffraction peak at $10.36^{\circ}$ (i.e., (020) according to the later structure solution) almost disappears, indicating that the long-range ordering of
BAC(10) has been almost destroyed at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As observed in Figure 1, the positions of most characteristic diffraction peaks of BAC(10) shift to a higher angle during the heating process, suggesting that the aluminoborate structure gradually shrinks during heating. Interestingly, the structure of BAC(10) can be regenerated by immersing the $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ calcined BAC(10) in deionized (DI) water, $0.0\dot{1}\mathrm{~M~NaCl}_{.}$ , or 0.01 M HCl solution, as shown by the PXRD patterns in Figure 1. The recovery process can be described as a “structural memory effect,” which has been often observed in LDHs.26 HCl solution gives the best recovery followed by NaCl solution and DI water. Structure recovery in DI water indicates that the adsorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules refill the void space after the removal of guest species in BAC(10) by calcination; a better structure recovery in NaCl solution implies that the resorption of $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ strengthens the filling effect; the best structure recovery in HCl solution suggests that the acidic environment will further facilitate the repair of the damage in the long-range ordering of BAC(10) due to dehydration at high temperatures.
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Figure 2a,b) and lowmagnification transmission electron microscopy (TEM, Figure
2c,d) images of the as-synthesized BAC(10) show that the product consists of aggregates of nanosheet-like hexagonshaped crystals with a length of $3-5\mu\mathrm{m}$ and a width of $0.{\overset{}{5}}-1$ $\mu\mathrm{m},$ , implying that BAC(10) might have a layered structure or the growth along the direction perpendicular to the sheet is significantly restrained. The corresponding atomic force microscopy (AFM) image (Figure 2e) shows that the average thickness of the nanosheet is about $4.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The energydispersive $\mathrm{x}$ -ray spectroscopy (EDS) mapping profile clearly reveals that the as-synthesized BAC(10) contains homogeneously distributed Al, B, O, and Cl elements (Figure 2f−i).
## Physicochemical Properties and Structural Analysis of Cr(VI)@BAC(10)
Anion exchange experiments were conducted at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using a batch sorption method, and the detailed information is provided in the Supporting Information. Figure 3a shows the PXRD patterns of BAC(10) before and after adsorption of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , which is denoted as $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ . PXRD data reveal that the main structure of BAC(10) is retained after the adsorption of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ . However, the crystallinity is obviously decreased. A TEM analysis on $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ (Figure S1) reveals that the lamellar morphology of BAC(10) is maintained after $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ adsorption. The EDS mapping profiles of BAC(10) and $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{VI}\big)\ @\mathrm{BAC}\big(10\big)$ also confirm that the adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ from aqueous solution follows an anion exchange process of $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ existing in the framework of BAC(10) (Figure S2). The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectrum of $\mathbf{\bar{C}r}(\mathrm{{VI}})@\mathrm{{BAC}}(10)$ shows an adsorption peak at $921~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ that is not present in the spectrum of BAC(10) (Figure 3b). This peak can be assigned to the antisymmetric $\dot{\mathrm{Cr}}^{\check{\Pi}}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ stretching vibration, confirming the incorporation of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ anions inside the structure of BAC(10).50 Figure 3c shows the $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of BAC(10) and $\mathbf{\tilde{Cr(VI)}}\mathbf{\@BAC(10)}$ . In Figure 3c, the peak from element Cl $(198.5~\mathrm{{eV},~\mathrm{{Cl}}~2p)}$ is clearly observed in the spectrum of BAC(10). After $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ adsorption, the Cl 2p peak at $198.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ completely disappears and a new $\mathrm{Cr~}2\mathrm{p}$ peak at about $580~\mathrm{eV}$ appears simultaneously. Figure 3d shows the high-resolution XPS spectra of the $\mathrm{Cr}2\mathsf{p}$ region. Compared to ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{CrO}}_{4},$ the Cr 2p peaks of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ are slightly blueshifted. The spectra of $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{VI}\big)\ @\mathrm{BAC}\big(10\big)$ have two main peaks, that is, $\mathrm{Cr~}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2}$ ( $589.5~\mathrm{eV})$ and Cr $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ $(579.7\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which correspond to $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{VI})}}$ . The minor signals with binding energies of 586.5 and $576.9\ \mathrm{~eV}$ are attributed to the trace amount of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ formed from the known reduction effects under X-ray irradiation.51 The TGA results show that the total weight loss of BAC(10) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{WI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ between 100 and $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is 33.3 and $26.1\%$ , respectively (Figure 3e). The TGA−MS analysis on BAC(10) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ (Figure 3f) shows that HCl is released from BAC(10) at above $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and no detectable HCl is released from $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ $\varpi\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ at the entire temperature range. The nitrogen adsorption analysis gives a BET surface area of $35.6~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10).$ , which is smaller than that of $59.3~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{\bar{g}}$ for as-synthesized BAC(10) (Figure S3), which is consistent with the decrease in crystallinity after the adsorption of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ (Figure 3a). Combining the above characterization results, it can be concluded that: (1) the $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ ions in the structure of BAC(10) are exchangeable; (2) $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ anions are incorporated inside the framework of BAC(10) via anion exchange; and (3) all $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ ions can be exchanged by $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ anions under such conditions.
The framework of $\mathbf{Cr}(\mathbf{\mathrm{VI}})@\mathbf{BAC}(1$ 0) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{0.22}{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{BO}_{4.3}\right.$ · $\left(\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}\right)_{0.22}{\cdot2.64\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}})$ has a cationic layered structure, built by ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedra, $\mathrm{BO}_{4}$ tetrahedra, and $\mathbf{BO}_{3}$ triangles. The ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedra are linked by edge- or corner-sharing to form a layer with exclusively six rings (Figure 5a). The six rings are decorated by either $\mathbf{BO}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{BO}_{4}$ . Three-coordinated boron atoms are found in the six rings with only one or two cornersharing $\mathrm{AlO}_{6}$ octahedra; each boron is coordinated to three edge-sharing oxygen atoms. Tetrahedrally coordinated boron atoms are located in the six rings with three corner-sharing ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedral pairs; each $\mathrm{BO}_{4}$ is connected to one edgesharing and two terminal oxygens in the ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedral layer, with one terminal oxygen pointing away from the layer (Figure 5a). There are two layers in each unit cell, and the layers are sinusoidal along the $c$ -axis (Figure 5b). Adjacent layers are held together by hydrogen bonding and form one-dimensional channels along the $a$ -axis. The ${\mathrm{BO}}_{4}$ tetrahedra extend toward the channel to serve as one of the chromate chemisorption sites. Within the channels are found three chromate ions, which are chemically bonded to the aluminoborate layer. One chromate ion (CrA, $77\%$ occupancy) is connected to an ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedron (Figure 5c), one $\left(\mathrm{CrB},\ 32\%\right)$ to both an ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedron and a ${\mathrm{BO}}_{4}$ tetrahedron, and the third chromate ion $(\mathrm{CrC},~24\%)$ is connected to two ${\mathrm{AlO}}_{6}$ octahedra, which are unusually formed during the ion-exchange process. The chromate ions are further stabilized via hydrogen bonding to the framework. There are in total 5.25 chromate ions per unit cell, which gives a formula of $\mathrm{H}_{0.22}{\cdot}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{BO}_{4.3}{\cdot}\left(\mathrm{HCrO}_{4}\right)_{0.22}$ · $2.64\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The chromate ions CrB and $\mathrm{CrC}$ are too close to each other and cannot exist simultaneously. If these sites are fully occupied, they could accommodate eight chromate ions. There are other sites with similar local environments as the binding sites, CrA, CrB, and $\mathrm{CrC}_{\mathrm{\tiny{\mathrm{~\tiny~C~r~C~}}}}$ , that may absorb additional chromate ions (Figure 5a). With the current cRED data, only three partially occupied chromate ion sites are found.
## Effect of pH on $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ Adsorption
Hydrolytic stability is important for the long-term usage of ion exchangers in aqueous solution. Figure S8 shows the PXRD patterns of BAC(10) immersed in solutions with various $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ under stirring, which indicates that BAC(10) has excellent hydrolytic stability in solutions with the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range from 2 to 10.5. When the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ reaches 12, obvious dissolution of BAC(10) and formation of crystalline $\mathrm{Al(OH)}_{3}$ (JCPDE card no. 20-0011) are observed. A further slight increase in $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ $\left(\mathrm{pH}=12.5\right)$ leads to the complete dissolution of BAC(10) and formation of highly crystalline $\mathrm{Al(OH)}_{3}$ . These results indicate that BAC(10) has excellent stability in acidic, neutral, and mildly basic solutions.
Figure 6 shows the influence of the initial $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ of the solution on the removal efficiency of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ and on the equilibrium $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ . When the initial $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ is increased from 2 to 10.5, the removal efficiency orderly decreases from 94.9 to $81.5\%$ (initial concentration of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ : $50~\mathrm{mg/L}$ and solid/liquid ratio: $2\mathrm{g/}$
L). Notably, the equilibrium $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ maintains in the range of 3.77 to 5.02 even though the initial $\mathsf{p H}$ ranges from 2 to 10.5, demonstrating the excellent acidic/basic buffer ability of BAC(10). This might be due to the exchange-in of $\mathrm{\Omega_{oH}-}$ and exchange-out of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ . When the initial $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ rises to 12, a sharp decline in $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal efficiency to $17.2\%$ and a dramatic increase in the equilibrium $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ to 10.95 are observed. The optimal removal efficiency for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ is $95.2\%$ when the initial solution $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ is 3.
In the solution containing $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , the predominant species of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ is $\mathrm{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ when the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ is less than 6.5 and $\hat{\mathrm{CrO}_{4}}^{2-}$ when the $\mathrm{\pH}$ is greater than 6.5 (Figure S9).53,54 For capturing one $\mathrm{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ anion, BAC(10) needs to release one $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , while two $C1^{-}$ anions will be needed to capture one $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ . Therefore, more $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ content in solution will lead to a lower removal efficiency. In this study, when the initial $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ is in the range of 2 to 10.5, the equilibrium $\mathsf{p H}$ is in the range of 3.77 to 5.02, at which the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ species mainly exists in the form of $\mathrm{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ (Figure S9). With the increase of $\mathrm{\tt{pH}},$ , the removal efficiency of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ slightly decreases (Figure 6), which is due to the competitive adsorption of more $\mathrm{\bar{OH}^{-}}$ on BAC(10). Further increasing the initial $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ from 10.5 to 12 results in the dramatic increase of the equilibrium $\mathsf{p H}$ from about 5.02 to 10.95 (Figure 6) at which $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ exists mainly in the form of $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ (Figure S9). In addition, the crystallinity of BAC(10) decreases significantly and $\mathrm{Al(OH)}_{3}$ starts to be formed at such $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ (Figure S8). Thus, a sudden drop in $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal efficiency is expected when the initial $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ reaches 12. It has been reported that cationic framework materials, such as metal oxides,55 cationic MOFs,56 LDHs,4 and $\mathrm{AERs},^{20}$ have shown strong dependence on the $\mathrm{\pH}$ of solution for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal and the optimal $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ for $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ adsorption of these materials is usually 3 to 5. Unlike these materials, BAC(10) exhibits a wide working $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ range from 2 to 10.5 and such a superior feature makes it promising in practical applications for $\mathrm{cr(\nablaI)}$ capture.
## Adsorption Kinetics and Adsorption Isotherms of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V}|)$ on BAC(10)
The adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ on BAC(10) is presented in Figure 7a (initial concentration of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ : $20{\mathrm{~mg/L}};$ solid/ liquid ratio: ${2\ {\mathrm{g}}/{\mathrm{L}};}$ and initial pH: 3). Meanwhile, we selected the commercial AER (IRN78) and a calcined layered double hydroxide (CLDH) for comparison. As shown in Figure 7a, at the contact time of $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the removal efficiency of BAC(10), IRN78, and CLDH is 66.9, 52.8, and $29.9\%$ , respectively. When the contact time is extended to $60~\mathrm{\min}$ , the corresponding removal efficiency is increased to 97.4, 97.9, and $69.5\%$ , respectively. On further extending the contact time, the removal efficiency of BAC(10) and IRN78 becomes almost constant, indicating that the adsorption equilibrium has been achieved at the contact time of $60~\mathrm{\min}$ . However, the adsorption equilibrium of CLDH can only be reached at the contact time of $150\ \mathrm{min}$ . The UV−vis spectra were acquired to monitor the concentration of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ in solution as a function of the contact time of 0, 10, 20, 40, 60, and $90~\mathrm{min}$ . As shown in Figure 7b, the intensity of the adsorption peak of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{7}{}^{2-}$ at $352~\mathrm{nm}$ in the UV−vis spectra of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\bar{V}I)}$ in aqueous solution at various adsorption times quickly decreases within only 10 min and does not show further decrease after $60~\mathrm{\min},$ suggesting that the anion-exchange process is completed. The inset photographs of Figure 7b also show that the solution changes to colorless within $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , indicating that BAC(10)
has a fast adsorption rate for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , which is consistent with the adsorption kinetics results and UV−vis analysis. Compared to the cationic MOFs, BAC(10) also exhibits an advance in the removal rate. For example, more than $24\mathrm{~h~}$ are needed for $\mathrm{ABT}{\cdot}2\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{23}$ 23 and $1{-}\dot{S}\dot{\mathrm{O}_{4}}{,}^{24}$ two MOFs with the fastest adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , to reach the adsorption equilibrium. The fast adsorption kinetics of BAC(10) makes it highly suitable for practical applications.
The adsorption kinetics of BAC(10) is fitted by the linear pseudo-first-order equation and linear pseudo-second-order equation (eqs S1 and S2), respectively. The kinetic parameters and the correlation coefficients $\left(R^{2}\right)$ are calculated on the basis of line regression (Figure $^{7\mathrm{c},\mathrm{d}},$ ) and are listed in Table S2. The fitting plot with the pseudo-second-order model shows a better linearity $\left(R^{2}\ =\ 0.{\dot{9}}99\right)$ compared to the pseudo-first-order model $\displaystyle{{\bf\ddot{R}}^{2}\ =\ 0.978})$ , which confirms that the adsorption kinetics of BAC(10) follows the pseudo-second-order model, that is, a chemical adsorption process. The fitting plots in Figures S10, S11 and the data in Table S2 show that the adsorption kinetics of IRN78 $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf\nabla}^{\prime}R^{2}=0.999,}\end{array}$ ) and CLDH $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{R}^{2}={\bf\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{\nabla}$ 0.997) also follows the pseudo-second-order model, which is consistent with the results previously reported.4,21
The adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ on BAC(10), CLDH, and IRN78 are illustrated in Figure 8a and the corresponding equilibrium $\mathsf{p H}$ values are plotted in Figure 8b (initial concentration of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}{:50{-}\bar{8}00\ \mathrm{mg/L}};$ solid/liquid ratio: 2 $\mathbf{g}/\mathrm{L};$ initial pH: 3; and contact time: 12 h under stirring). Both Langmuir and Freundlich models (eqs S3 and S4) are used to fit the experimental data and the results are presented in Figure 8c,d, respectively. The adsorption parameters and the correlation coefficients $\left(R^{2}\right)$ on the basis of line regression (Figure 8c,d) are listed in Table S3. The fitting plots in Figure $^{8\mathrm{c},\mathrm{d}}$ and the data in Table S3 clearly show that the Langmuir model provides a better description of the adsorption behavior of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ than the Freundlich model does, indicating a monolayer coverage of $\mathrm{{Cr}(W I)}$ anions on the internal surface of BAC(10), CLDH, and IRN-78. According to the Langmuir fitting, the maximum adsorption capacity $\left(q_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ for ${\mathrm{Cr}}({\bar{\mathrm{{M}}}})$ on BAC(10) is $139.1\ \mathrm{mg/g},$ which is much higher than those on
CLDH $\left(81.43\mathrm{\mg/g}\right)$ and IRN78 $\left(62.77~\mathrm{mg/g}\right)$ , consistent with the previously reported values for CLDH and IRN78.48,57
Table S4 summarizes the maximum adsorption capacities for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ adsorption on typical anion exchangers such as cationic MOFs, LDHs, and AERs. Obviously, the maximum adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ on BAC(10) has outperformed most of them and is just less than that of MOFs of MOR-2.58 The results in Figure 8b show that the equilibrium solutions containing BAC(10) are acidic $\left(\mathrm{pH}\ =\ 4{-}4.5\right)$ , while those containing CLDH or IRN78 are basic $\mathrm{\Phi}^{'}\mathrm{pH}=9.5\mathrm{-}11.6\$ ) even though they have the same initial $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ of 3.0. As discussed above, the distribution of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ species is strongly affected by the solution $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ . The $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ species mainly exists in the form of $\mathrm{CrO}_{4}^{2-}$ in the basic medium $\mathrm{(pH~>~}6.5)$ and thus the adsorption capacity of the anion exchanger will be seriously inhibited. This is an important reason why BAC(10) has higher adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ than IRN78 and CLDH.
## Adsorption Selectivity, Removal Efficiency, and Recyclability of BAC(10)
In addition to $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ , polluted water usually contains other types of anions such as $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ . The existence of such anions apparently has a negative influence on the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ due to competitive adsorption. Hence, efficient removal of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ from waste water is still a great challenge in the presence of large excess of competing anions. Here, we investigated the removal efficiency of BAC(10) on $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ in the presence of competitive anions of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{HCO_{3}^{-},F^{-}}.$ , or $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ at various concentrations and the results are shown in Figure $9\mathrm{a}$ . The initial concentration of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ is $20.8~\mathrm{\mg/L}~(\mathrm{0.4~\mM}).$ , and the concentration of competitive anions is 0, 0.4, 2, 4, or $8~\mathrm{mM}$ . The solid/liquid ratio is $2{\mathrm{~g/L}}$ . To exclude the interference of $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}_{\mathrm{\tt{f}}}$ , all the initial $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ values are adjusted to 7. The results in Figure 9a show that the monovalent anions of ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Br}^{-}.$ , $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ , and ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ have negligible influence on the removal efficiency of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ even under 20 times $\left(8\mathrm{mM}\right)$ ) excess dose. The removal efficiency only decreases by $12.5\%$ at the maximum even in the presence of 20 times excess of $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{\theta}}_{8\mathrm{mM}})$ . Unlike monovalent anions, a large amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ can adversely affect the adsorption of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ and the removal efficiency decreased by
$20.8\%$ in the presence of $8\ \mathrm{\mM\CO_{3}}^{2-}$ (20 times excess). Although the presence of large excess of $\mathrm{CO}_{3}^{2-}$ has a relative obviously negative influence on the removal efficiency of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{{Cr}(W I)}$ , it is still obviously superior to many other anion exchangers, such as $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{Mg}/\mathrm{Al}.\mathrm{LD}\mathrm{\bar{H}s}^{59}$ and UiO${66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@}$ silica.60 This may be ascribed to the excellent acidic/ basic buffer ability of BAC(10) as described above. These results indicate that BAC(10) can selectively capture $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ even in the presence of large excess of competitive anions.
Although many adsorbents have excellent adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ , it is difficult to reduce moderate and trace levels of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ below the maximum allowable discharge standard defined by the WHO $\left(0.05~\mathrm{{\mg/L}}\right)$ .6 Figure 9b presents the residual $\mathrm{{Cr}(W I)}$ concentration of the solution treated by BAC(10) with a solid/liquid ratio of $2~\mathrm{g/L}$ . The initial concentration varied from 2.5 to $20~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The results show that the residual $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ concentration of the solution is lower than $0.05~\mathrm{mg/L}$ after the treatment of BAC(10) if the initial concentration of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ does not exceed $10\ \mathrm{\mg/L},$ which is below the allowed safe levels of WHO for drinking water as well as China and European Union. These results demonstrate that BAC(10) has excellent removal efficiency for low $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ concentration solutions, which makes it a promising material in practical applications for deep treatment of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{\nabla{U})}$ -contaminated water. Combining the outstanding selectivity and depth removal performance of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ and the physicochemical and structural properties of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{WI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10).$ , we can conclude that the adsorption mechanism is based on the following: (i) ion exchange as the main mechanism of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ removal; (ii) formation of chemical bonds between the partial chromate ions and the inorganic sheet; and (iii) electrostatic interactions between the $\mathrm{Cr(\dot{V}I)}$ anions and positively charged framework of BAC(10).
Furthermore, the recyclability of BAC(10) was investigated. The $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})@\mathrm{BAC}(10)$ samples could be regenerated by a facile ion-exchange process method in a NaCl solution (detailed information is provided in the Supporting Information). Figure 9c shows the removal efficiency of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ in five cycles of adsorption−desorption process. After the first cycle, the removal efficiency decreases from 95.2 to $90.3\%$ . Even at the fifth adsorption−desorption process, the removal efficiency of $70.3\%$ can still be reached. The PXRD patterns of regenerated BAC(10) samples show that the structure is retained after multiple cycles (Figure S12). The decrease in removal efficiency with the increase in the number of cycles might be due to the strong affinity and the formation of chemical bonding between partial $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ and the framework of BAC(10), which leads to the incomplete replacement of $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ anions with $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ in the regeneration process. The EDS spectra and photographs of the regenerated BAC(10) samples further demonstrate the existence of undesorbed $\bar{\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})}$ in the regenerated BAC(10) (Figures S13, S14).
In addition to $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ , many pollutants inherently exist in their oxyanion forms, such as $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{MoO_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-}$ . Among these oxyanions, $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ is employed as the surrogate of $\mathrm{\bar{TcO_{4}}}^{-}$ $(^{99}\mathrm{Tc})$ , which is a highly problematic radioactive waste;61 $\mathrm{{H_{2}P O_{4}}^{-}}$ is one of the main causes for water eutrophication; $\mathbf{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO}_{4}^{2-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO}_{4}^{3-}$ are also harmful oxyanions to human health. $62-{\dot{6}}4$ The removal efficiency of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO_{4}}^{3-}$ is shown in Figure 9d. The initial concentrations of Re, Mo, Se, P, and $\mathrm{v}$ were $20~\mathrm{mg/L}$ . The results show that the removal efficiency of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO_{4}}^{3-}$ is over $99\%$ , while for $\mathrm{ReO}_{4}^{-}$ , it is relatively low $(29.1\%)$ . The results clearly show that BAC(10) also has excellent removal ability for $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO_{4}}^{3-}$ .
## CONCLUSIONS
Highly crystalline aluminoborate BAC(10) with a pure inorganic 2D cationic framework is hydrothermally synthesized with cost-effective source materials, and the structure of chromate ion-exchanged BAC(10) has been solved by cRED. The anion exchange property of BAC(10) for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ from aqueous solution is superior to the commercial anion resin and LDHs with the maximum adsorption capacity and adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ . Owing to the acidic/basic buffer ability, BAC(10) exhibits a broad working $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ range of $2{-}10.5$ and excellent selectivity for $\mathrm{{Cr}(\mathrm{{W})}}$ even in the presence of large amounts of excessive competitive anions. In addition, BAC(10) exhibits outstanding selectivity and depth removal performance for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ . The investigation on structural analysis and adsorption mechanism confirms that the removal of $\dot{\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}}$ from aqueous solution is achieved via the anion exchange of $\mathrm{HCrO_{4}}^{-}$ with freely mobile $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ inside the structure of BAC(10) and formation of chemical bonds between the partial chromate ions and the inorganic sheet, which results in excellent selectivity for $\mathrm{{Cr}(V I)}$ . The excellent performance and low cost make BAC(10) a promising material in wastewater treatment for $\mathrm{{Cr}(\nabla I)}$ removal. BAC(10) also exhibits excellent removal efficiency for other harmful oxyanions such as $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SeO_{4}}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}{}^{-}$ , and $\mathrm{VO_{4}}^{3-}$ from aqueous solution. To further investigate the possibility of use of BAC(10) in practical applications, we will conduct large-scale synthesis of BAC(10) and manufacture it into macroscopic structures such as granules, pellets, cylinders, or membranes to meet the requirement for practical applications.
